WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.669
How do you process a hundred and twenty-eight gigabytes of data on a machine with only sixteen gigabytes of RAM?

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:09.337
The same way Unix did it in the seventies.

00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:13.006
You don't load all hundred and twenty-eight gigabytes into RAM.

00:00:13.006 --> 00:00:16.675
You reed sixty-four megabytes at a time.

00:00:16.675 --> 00:00:20.344
The disk hands you a fixed-size page on every IO.

00:00:20.344 --> 00:00:24.012
One byte or all sixty-four megabytes, same cost.

00:00:24.012 --> 00:00:27.681
So algorithms count pages, not bytes.

00:00:27.681 --> 00:00:31.907
Start with the picture.

00:00:31.907 --> 00:00:36.133
The CPU on the left never touches the SSD.

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:40.359
It works out of its registers and its cashes, L one, L two, L three.

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:44.585
The hardware fills those cashes from RAM in sixty-four byte cash lines, in nanoseconds.

00:00:44.585 --> 00:00:48.811
Pages live one level down.

00:00:48.811 --> 00:00:53.037
The SSD on the right holds all hundred and twenty-eight gigabytes, two thousand and forty-eight sixty-four megabyte pages.

00:00:53.037 --> 00:00:57.263
RAM in the middle holds only two hundred and fifty-six of those same pages.

00:00:57.263 --> 00:01:01.489
To work on data, you page it in.

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:05.715
One block crosses from SSD to RAM.

00:01:05.715 --> 00:01:09.941
That is one IO.

00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:14.167
And even on a fast SSD it is transfer-bound.

00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:18.393
Moving sixty-four megabytes at five gigabytes a second takes about thirteen milliseconds, whether you wanted one row or the whole block.

00:01:18.393 --> 00:01:22.619
When RAM fills, you evict a page and load the next.

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:26.845
That is the whole trick.

00:01:26.845 --> 00:01:31.071
All two thousand pages stream through two hundred and fifty-six slots, one page at a time.

00:01:31.071 --> 00:01:34.159
Why go to all this trouble?

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:37.248
Because the gap is impossible to feel in nanoseconds.

00:01:37.248 --> 00:01:40.336
So rescale it.

00:01:40.336 --> 00:01:43.424
If one cache hit took one second, then RAM would be a minute and a half away.

00:01:43.424 --> 00:01:46.512
An SSD read, an afternoon.

00:01:46.512 --> 00:01:49.600
A hard disk read, four months.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.689
That is what paging fights.

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:55.777
It keeps your working set in RAM, so a read is a short pause, not a whole season.

00:01:55.777 --> 00:01:58.558
The hierarchy goes like this.

00:01:58.558 --> 00:02:01.338
CPU registers and cashes at the top, nanoseconds.

00:02:01.338 --> 00:02:04.119
RAM in the middle, hundreds of nanoseconds.

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:06.900
SSD next, microseconds.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.680
Spinning disk, milliseconds.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.461
Network storage at the bottom.

00:02:12.461 --> 00:02:15.241
Each tier is about a hundred times slower than the one above.

00:02:15.241 --> 00:02:18.022
And each is about ten times cheaper per byte.

00:02:18.022 --> 00:02:21.327
The cost model is simple.

00:02:21.327 --> 00:02:24.632
One IO fetches one page.

00:02:24.632 --> 00:02:27.937
A page is a fixed size, typically sixty-four megabytes in big-data systems.

00:02:27.937 --> 00:02:31.242
The cost of an algorithm is the pages it reeds plus the pages it writes.

00:02:31.242 --> 00:02:34.547
Comparisons, loops, arithmetic.

00:02:34.547 --> 00:02:37.852
Next to a disk reed, those are free.

00:02:37.852 --> 00:02:41.156
Today's hardware does not change the rule.

00:02:41.156 --> 00:02:44.460
A modern GPU does ten teraflops.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.764
But feeding it data still goes through the same storage hierarchy.

00:02:47.764 --> 00:02:51.067
The ten-million-times gap is still there.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:54.371
CPUs and GPUs are starving for data.

00:02:54.371 --> 00:02:57.675
The storage tier is what feeds them.

00:02:57.675 --> 00:03:00.979
So even modern AI workloads page data the same way classical databases did.

00:03:00.979 --> 00:03:04.044
Paging is the foundation under everything in this module.

00:03:04.044 --> 00:03:07.109
Hashing, indexing, sorting, joining.

00:03:07.109 --> 00:03:10.174
Every one of those algorithms gets measured in pages.

00:03:10.174 --> 00:03:13.239
Drive that count down and the algorithm gets faster.

00:03:13.239 --> 00:03:16.304
Drive it up and no amount of CPU helps.

00:03:16.304 --> 00:03:19.369
Next we make this counting precise.

00:03:19.369 --> 00:03:22.434
The IO cost model.
